% ChatGPT plan runtime and VM transfer
Own pdd/chatgpt_runtime.py. Use credential store, OAuth request/verification ports,
chatgpt_oauth and Python built-ins. No new dependency. Official contracts:
https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/profiles-and-sessions
https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/codex-app-server
https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/self-hosted-vms
R1: access_record locks registration and checks owner/grant. Near expiry refresh
with issued client ID, refresh_token, resource, no scope; observe earliest_refresh_at.
Save rotating token/expiry/grant atomically. Validate replacement ID if returned.
Omitted scope retains grant. Terminal errors clear unusable tokens, retain identity
and client. Temporary failures preserve credentials. Concurrent processes never race.
R2: app_server holds a shared usage lease for its child lifetime, but releases the
short credential lock after access_record. Several same-account processes can run.
Refresh is serialized independently. Transfer takes exclusive usage before any SSH.
app_server uses supplied tested codex binary and existing CODEX_HOME. Set child
ACCESS_TOKEN only. Documented openai_chatgpt_plan provider: api.openai.com/v1,
responses, requires_openai_auth=false, supports_websockets=false, env_key=ACCESS_TOKEN.
Relay stdio directly. No argv tokens, auth.json changes, parent environment changes.
Remove inherited API/GitHub credentials and SSH agent. Host owns initialize,
initialized and safe restart/thread/resume near token expiry.
R3: Prepare VM host ID first, then transfer only selected registration using SSH argv
and stdin, no shell. Fixed Python remote commands. Reject unsafe/option destinations.
Preserve VM host ID; fence source before import. Uncertain SSH leaves source fenced.
Successful transfer clears source tokens; VM owns refresh. Mac needs a distinct session.
R4: No account activation or live test. Catalog is not entitlement proof. Live completed
turn, thread resume and encrypted reasoning limitations remain explicitly unverified.
R5: Public runtime entry functions require PDD_CHATGPT_SIGN_IN=1 before side effects.
R6: Pass the shared lease file descriptor to the Codex child explicitly. If the
wrapper is killed, the child retains transfer exclusion until it exits. Host still
must inventory unmanaged processes. Tests prove inherited lease behavior with a
real offline child; no tested live Codex behavior is inferred from that fake.
R7: Default child runner is parent-owned. Forward TERM/INT to the owned Codex child,
wait for exit and escalate to kill after three seconds. Restore parent handlers.
Close/EOF must not silently abandon a live child. SIGKILL cannot run parent cleanup;
inherited usage lease still protects a surviving child and H must inventory it.
